 "transcript": {
  "answer": "Victoria's job market for women has weakened over the past year. The figures below are ABS seasonally adjusted estimates for September 2026.\n\n**Women in Victoria, September 2026**\n- **Unemployment rate:** 5.1%. It was 4.0–4.2% through most of the second half of 2025, for example 4.16% in July 2025 and 3.98% in October 2025. It rose through 2026 to a peak of 5.24% in July and has eased slightly since.\n- **Participation rate:** 63.6%, down from 63.9% in July 2025. It dipped to 62.4% in April 2026 and has partly recovered.\n- **Employment-to-population ratio:** 60.4%, down from 61.2% in July 2025. It fell to 59.5% in April 2026.\n- **Full-time employment:** about 1,008,000 women, down from about 1,038,000 in July 2025, a fall of roughly 29,000. It bottomed near 992,000 in May 2026.\n\n**Compared with men in Victoria**\n- Men's unemployment rate was 5.0% in September 2026 and has stayed around 5% for the past year. It was 5.0% in July 2025, so it has barely changed.\n- Men's participation rate was 71.5%, essentially unchanged.\n- Women's unemployment was about a point below men's a year ago. It is now slightly higher (5.1% against 5.0%).\n\n**Summary:** Most of the deterioration in Victoria's labour market over this period shows up in women's figures. Conditions have steadied since the mid-2026 low point, and participation and the employment ratio have recovered a little. Unemployment is still about a percentage point above where it was a year ago.\n\nThese state-level monthly figures are volatile, since the survey sample is small. A move of a few tenths of a point from one month to the next isn't very meaningful. I couldn't retrieve part-time employment, so the full-time fall may be partly offset by part-time work.",
